{
	title: 'The event loop never blocks over 10 ms',
	spec: `Hal never freezes. No piece of work on the host or on a client (terminal or web) holds the event loop for more than 10 ms: work that can grow with history size or tab count (laying out and painting history, writing a big frame to the terminal, decoding history pages, building marks indexes, replaying a session, loading tabs, reading or writing files on a hot path) runs asynchronously or in slices that yield to the scheduler between them, and nothing spins a core. Keys, frames and host events keep flowing while such work runs; it may finish later, never block.

The rule is measured, not assumed: perf.watch (common/perf.ts) runs a timer every 5 ms and reports each time it fires more than 10 ms late. main starts it when HAL_STALLS names a file and appends one line per block, pid, wall-clock ms and length ms. scripts/perf (task te) sets HAL_STALLS for every process it launches, prints per setup (host cold and warm, peer, -r, tab switching, web page load) the count, longest and total of blocks over 10 ms with the worst ones and when they happened, and fails if any block exceeds 10 ms. Garbage-collection pauses cannot be told apart and count too: holding less memory is how they are kept short. Histories are streamed line by line (asonl); single ASON files stay small (about 1 MB at most), so parsing one is not sliced.`,
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		"Baseline before any fix (generated home scale 1, load ~2): blocks over 10 ms per setup, count/longest/total: host cold 11/674/1457 ms, host warm 17/518/981, peer 8/755/1037, -r 16/629/1153, tab switching 23/111/497, web page load (host) 3/16/40. The long ones come about 2-3 s after start: the focused 50 MB tab's backfill drawn into scrollback in one go.",
		'Terminal client slicing (frame.layout deadline, render past/tail, terminal.writer async output, backfill.apply without stringifying all pages). scripts/perf on the same generated home, before -> after, count/longest/total ms: host cold 7/672/1413 -> 8/668/814, host warm 9/511/817 -> 9/73/244, peer 23/515/1238 -> 8/51/148, -r 9/574/796 -> 3/49/86, tab switching 33/1120/1837 -> 7/39/119, web page load 1/526/526 -> 3/62/102. Key p99 went from 558-679 ms to 77-136 ms; what remains of it is key frames queued behind a multi-megabyte repaint the pty reader consumes slowly. What is left over 10 ms is host-side (startup, pages/marks answering in-process: host cold 668 ms at 0.7 s is the host) plus about 10-12 ms per key in the in-process host draft path (drafts.flush -> host act -> broadcast/save). Profile with HAL_STALLS set, not by grouping CPU-profile samples: sliced work with 1 ms gaps looks like one long run in the profile.',
		"Host side (pages.ts steps + slices, host holds a client's commands for a session while its open is read, tabs' states computed in slices before the tabs event, wire.ts skips copying history records, status caches derived states). scripts/perf, count/longest/total ms, after the terminal slicing (fe1f1c7) -> after this: host cold 8/668/814 -> 7/33/119, host warm 9/73/244 -> 4/46/113, peer 8/51/148 -> 6/17/75, -r 3/49/86 -> 4/18/54, tab switching 7/39/119 -> 1/13/13, web page load 3/62/102 -> 5/22/70. ASON parsing itself is the cost of decoding (ason.ts is verbatim, task mw): a 1 MB string field takes ~5 ms (an intermediate host-only run on 18076d7 cut host cold from 10/676/1506 with the terminal repaint still whole). Left: the host warm 46 ms at 0.1 s and 20-35 ms at startup are process start (module load, config/liveFile reads, first frame) plus GC; a single record of several MB still costs its own decode (~5-10 ms per 2 MB) since one record is never split; the socket/WebSocket path still stringifies a page in one go (adapt: ~8-20 ms for a 4 MB page); ~10 ms per key in the in-process draft path is untouched.",
		'Key-output investigation at 61e7449 (scale 1 generated home): two clean runs p99/max ms, host cold 82/170 and 85/113; host warm 206/206 and 335/335; peer 223/223 and 61/61; -r 227/227 and 111/111. Cold longest stalls were 835 and 342 ms. CPU profiling reproduced 415 ms in turnRecovery.recover -> leftWork -> pages.marks -> drive(catchUp): the 1 s first-frame fallback starts recovery before sliced tab indexing finishes. This race also exists at 3652846; that revision ran cold longest 33 ms when indexing beat the fallback. Recovery now awaits sliced marks catch-up first. After this change on 61e7449: cold p99/max 52/108 ms, warm 88/88, peer 119/119, -r 126/126; tab-switch max 24 ms; cold longest stall 46 ms. A one-time forced-immediate startup fallback probe gave key p99/max 37/37 and longest stall 36 ms. Full test passed 1267 tests, tsc and lint. An async local transport experiment did not improve measured key latency and was removed. The remaining key latency is still ordered terminal-output backlog, synchronous draft persistence and startup/GC/catalog work; no claim that the 50 ms key or 10 ms event-loop goals are met.',
		'Safe repaint preemption is not a writer queue-priority change: paintParts advances prev/cursorRow to the completed future frame, and SYNC_ON spans the whole repaint. Chunks can end inside escape sequences, UTF-8 or soft-wrapped URL chains. A future progressive repaint must expose complete screen-consistent checkpoints with known physical cursor/style/link/sync state, keep a live prompt/footer independent of history scrollback, and compute later diffs from actually committed screen state. Cap bytes in flight and test final screen, scrollback and link/soft-wrap equality against an uninterrupted canonical repaint. Existing writer already waits for each 64 KiB async write callback; smaller FIFO pieces alone cannot make the prompt bypass megabytes. Quit/suspend currently synchronously flush queued repaint output but cannot wait for the write already in flight: restoration/park bytes may overtake or interleave that last chunk. A slow terminal may delay quitting; quitting during repaint can leave partial/stale rows, wrong cursor or an incomplete escape/mode sequence until the shell resets it. Suspend can show the same issue until resume forces a redraw. Frequency depends on quitting during a large slow repaint; no measured rate. Recovery slicing does not alter terminal output or this risk.',
		"Run 04-irc (load 3-5 on 4 cores, scale 1; commits 4d4b173, aba783a, 0a67830, e04845a). scripts/perf blocks over 10 ms, count/longest/total ms, baseline at cd2629f -> after e04845a: host cold 451/348/12093 -> 47/636/1672, host warm 280/336/7942 -> 57/324/1370, peer 312/90/9092 -> 72/259/1514, -r 266/79/9251 -> 65/340/1279, tab switching 246/51/4247 -> 12/47/195, web page load 89/61/1974 -> 7/93/213. The hundreds of blocks were a regression since the previous run: frame.layout re-ran promptChanges.group and frame.order (measuring each multi-call batch's rows) over the whole history on every call, and sliced layout calls it every 5 ms slice, so the host's terminal spun a core (26 s CPU in 37 s); now memoized per transcript items array. Also fixed: models.dev's 5 MB answer parsed/picked/cached in a worker (models-dev-worker.ts; ~100 ms on the host thread), its cache one provider per line parsed on first use (~10-20 ms at greet), promptFiles.start checks open tabs in 4 ms slices (~50-90 ms), weekday without Intl, tabs.indexed yields every 8th tab (an all-small-reads pages.slices never yields; making slices itself time-bound broke lazy-history equality, and yielding on every tab made one-tab connect async and broke greetings.test). Longest/worst remaining are single huge blocks: host cold ~640 ms at ~0.7 s and ~250-340 ms at 2-4 s in every setup (not yet attributed; earlier notes point at the focused tab's backfill repaint), getDay/Intl timezone init on first date use (~8 ms, one-time), ASON decode of single records in snapshotSteps, tab-bar fit (colors.project per tab) 10-20 ms per frame, and the in-process draft path (drafts.edit/flush -> host act -> broadcast) 15-25 ms under load. Method: BUN_OPTIONS='--cpu-prof --cpu-prof-dir=DIR' on scripts/perf profiles every process; align stalls.log lines (pid, wall ms end, length) with profile sample times (startTime is epoch µs) to attribute each block; stalls in the long-running host count under peer/-r/tab switching. Under load many 10-30 ms blocks have far fewer samples than ms: CPU contention, not Hal.",
		"Run 06-hit (scale 1; load 2-5 on 4 cores from other agents, so many 10-70 ms blocks have far fewer CPU samples than ms: contention). scripts/perf had broken: /auth now prints 'Login code (...): X' plus a ?auth= link; run.ts reads the link (bf43f04, task te). Two fixes (31ce565, b2a1eb1): frame.layout's batchOrder.order (it lays out every multi-call batch to test fits) ran over the whole freshly backfilled history in one go, ~300 ms in every setup at 2-4 s; it now runs in slices under layout's deadline and resumes from frame.state.ordered, and render.tail's unsaved layout no longer overwrites that memo (which forced a full reorder). The 650 ms host cold block at 0.7 s was historyMigration.run reading and searching every history synchronously when state/migrations.ason is absent (any generated or fresh home); it now scans each history 1 MB at a time with async reads and parses only one that mentions 'ask'. scripts/perf count/longest/total ms, two runs before -> three runs after: host cold 18/568/1090, 23/661/1356 -> 65/50/1184, 18/43/348, 36/42/639; host warm 20/215/594, 25/309/767 -> 37/74/773, 22/36/403, 51/69/1053; peer 34/230/777, 45/317/1070 -> 79/70/1291, 25/56/482, 109/65/2305; -r 31/303/789, 50/307/1083 -> 47/41/765, 28/40/450, 158/70/2726; tab switching 9/24/128, 8/40/153 -> 17/32/274, 8/27/132, 22/24/324; web page load 2/53/70, 7/38/142 -> 1/18/18, 3/30/61, 6/32/119 (counts swing with load; the third run was profiled). No single block over ~75 ms remains. Profiled leftovers, each 30-70 ms under load: backfill.apply -> transcript.prepend/fromSnapshot on one page (~25 samples), pages.pageSteps decoding/raw toString of single records, host.release/answer -> tab-switch.focusOn at greet, promptFiles.catchUp -> promptTrail.check and system-prompt date/getDay at startup, turnRecovery.recover and web.start/version git spawn at startup.",
		'2026-10-06, one-off run against a copy of a real home (262 sessions, 2.2 GB, 25 MB largest history): blocks of 700-740 ms on host cold and warm start (at ~1.1-1.5 s, ~2 s, ~3.6 s) that generated data (scripts/perf, scale 1) never shows; its worst was 35 ms. Interactive: cold 317 ms, warm 218 ms, peer 169 ms, -r 145 ms. Generated homes miss whatever real histories cost; find the 700 ms block with real data.',
		"Run 06-jab, real-home copy (262 sessions, 2.2 GB, 106 open tabs; load 0.4-1.5 on 4 cores; commits 375984d, f586c5e). The 700-740 ms blocks were one cause: marks.ason kept every changed path (changedPaths) and one bash call had declared a whole tree, so one open tab's marks held 130k paths (15 MB; others 0.1-13 MB). Loading it while tabs are indexed cost ~800 ms in one go (ASON parse 216 ms plus liveFile's eager synced stringify 595 ms), and every append to that session re-stringified it (~600 ms). Marks now keep only the count (files); changed-files.ts rebuilds the paths in memory from the indexed file_changes records when a new one needs them (one decode of those records per host run, e.g. ~440 ms for a 25 MB record, only when that session records a new change). Old marks convert on first load: a one-time block of about the old load cost per big unopened session. Profiled host start before -> after, longest/total: 713/1166 ms -> 51/414 ms. realperf (scripts/perf's probes on the copy), interactive cold/warm/peer/-r before 317/218/169/145 ms -> two runs 206/188/142/131 and 178/167/156/133 ms; blocks over 10 ms count/longest/total: host cold 25/71/701 and 11/50/275, warm 21/48/399 and 9/42/224, peer 9/47/151 and 8/44/140, -r 19/35/292 and 19/34/296, web page load 1/15/15 and 2/25/36 (tab switching not exercised: no open-tab list). Left, all in the first 0.4 s and with no single owner (samples fewer than ms, partly contention/GC): process start and init (prompt-files catch-up incl. first getDay timezone init, terminal.available, config/tabs liveFile reads), greet listing 106 tabs synchronously (status.stateOf, sessions.open liveFiles, tab-bar fit/colors.project), turnRecovery.recover and busy.drop, web.watchSource's recursive fs.watch, single-record ASON decodes in tab indexing and snapshot pages. Goal not met: interactive ~130-210 ms, blocks 30-70 ms.",
		"Deleted host.test.ts 'a snapshot read in slices misses no record appended meanwhile…' (2026-10-06): it failed in the full suite under load and passed alone, so it was flaky (AGENTS.md: delete flaky tests). The sliced-snapshot invariant it covered now has no test."
	],
	needs: ['te']
}
